.TH "CHECKSUM" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
checksum \- hash a file, or check it against a hash or SUMS file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
checksum [options] file ...
checksum [options] file hash
checksum [options] -c sums-file
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBchecksum\fR answers one question after a download. Is this file the one the project published? It works three ways.
.PP
With only file names, it hashes each file with sha256. Before that, it looks beside the file for a SUMS file that lists it, such as the \fBSHA256SUMS\fR next to a Debian ISO. When it finds one, it checks the file against it and prints \fBOK\fR or \fBFAILED\fR instead of a bare hash. When there is none, it prints the hash in the same format as \fBsha256sum\fR, so you can save the output as a SUMS file of your own.
.PP
With a file and a hash, such as one you copied from a download page, it compares the two. You do not have to say which algorithm the hash is. \fBchecksum\fR works it out from the length.
.PP
With \fB\-c SUMS\fR, it checks every file the SUMS file lists and prints one line per file with a count at the end.
.PP
Every check prints one line per file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
OK       debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso
FAILED   debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-1.iso
MISSING  debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-2.iso
.fi
.RE
.PP
In a terminal, \fBOK\fR is green and the other two are red. \fBchecksum\fR exits 1 when any file fails or is missing, so a script can stop on a bad download.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-algo NAME\fR
Use md5, sha1, sha256, sha512, b2 or b3. The default is sha256. \fBblake2b\fR, \fBsha256sum\fR and upper case names work too.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-check FILE\fR
Check every file listed in a SUMS file.
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-missing\fR
With \fB\-c\fR, skip listed files that are not here instead of calling them missing.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-sums\fR
Print hashes only. Never look for a SUMS file.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Show only the result lines.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Show each step, the SUMS file it found, and each hash command before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH HOW IT PICKS THE ALGORITHM
.PP
For \fBchecksum FILE HASH\fR, the length of the hash decides.
.TP
32
md5
.TP
40
sha1
.TP
64
sha256, then b3 when \fBb3sum\fR is installed
.TP
128
sha512, then b2
.PP
Two algorithms make 64 digit hashes, and two make 128 digit ones. \fBchecksum\fR tries the common one first. When that does not match, it tries the other, and \fB\-v\fR shows \fBno match with sha256, trying b3\fR. The result line names the algorithm that matched.
.PP
A pasted hash may start with a label such as \fBsha256:\fR or \fBSHA512:\fR, the way Docker and many download pages print it. \fBchecksum\fR reads the label as \fB\-a\fR and drops it. It also drops spaces, so a hash split across two lines still works when you quote it.
.PP
For a SUMS file, the choice goes in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-a\fR, when you give it.
.IP \(bu 2
The tag on a BSD style line, such as \fBSHA256 (file.iso) = ...\fR. Fedora and FreeBSD use this format.
.IP \(bu 2
The name of the SUMS file, such as \fBSHA512SUMS\fR or \fBfile.iso.sha256\fR, when the hash has the right length for it.
.IP \(bu 2
The length of the hash, as above.
.SH HOW IT FINDS A SUMS FILE
.PP
For each file, \fBchecksum\fR looks for these files beside it, in this order, and uses the first one that lists the file by name:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfile.sha256\fR, \fBfile.sha256sum\fR, and the same with sha512, b2, sha1, md5 and b3.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA256SUMS\fR, \fBSHA512SUMS\fR, \fBB2SUMS\fR, \fBSHA1SUMS\fR, \fBMD5SUMS\fR and \fBB3SUMS\fR, each also as \fBNAME.txt\fR and in lower case as \fBsha256sums.txt\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Files ending in \fBCHECKSUM\fR or \fB\-CHECKSUMS\fR, which is how Fedora names them.
.PP
A \fBfile.sha256\fR that holds only a bare hash, with no name after it, counts as the hash for \fBfile\fR. With \fB\-a\fR, only the SUMS files for that algorithm count.
.PP
It reads three line formats:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ff0b18e3...  debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso        GNU, from sha256sum
ff0b18e3... *debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso        GNU, binary mode
SHA256 (Fedora-Workstation-Live-42-1.1.x86_64.iso) = 26551053...   BSD
.fi
.RE
.PP
Blank lines, lines that start with \fB#\fR, and Windows line endings are fine. The PGP signature lines around a Fedora CHECKSUM file are skipped, because they match none of the formats.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBchecksum\fR only reads. It never changes, moves or deletes a file.
.IP \(bu 2
It feeds each file to the hash tool on standard input, so a file named \fB\-c\fR or with a newline in its name cannot change what the tool does.
.IP \(bu 2
A hash of the wrong length is a usage error with exit 2. It never counts as a failed file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBchecksum\fR does not check the PGP signature on a SUMS file. When the SUMS file comes from the same mirror as the ISO, a hacked mirror can change both. For that, run \fBgpg \-\-verify SHA256SUMS.sign SHA256SUMS\fR first.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
\fBchecksum\fR takes no options for the hash tools. Everything after \fB\-\-\fR is a file name, which lets you hash a file whose name starts with a dash.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ checksum -- -weird-name.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBsha256sum\fR, \fBmd5sum\fR, \fBsha1sum\fR, \fBsha512sum\fR and \fBb2sum\fR, all from coreutils. BusyBox has all of them except \fBb2sum\fR, so on Alpine \fB\-a b2\fR needs \fBapk add coreutils\fR. \fBb3\fR needs \fBb3sum\fR, which is a package called \fBb3sum\fR on Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, Arch and Alpine. When it is missing, \fBchecksum\fR names the package and exits 3.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Check a download against the SUMS file beside it
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ls
SHA256SUMS  debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso
$ checksum debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso
OK       debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso  (sha256, SHA256SUMS)
.fi
.RE
.SS A download that went wrong
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ checksum debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-1.iso
FAILED   debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-1.iso  (sha256, SHA256SUMS)
  want c36abfca184c4d3b5458a864bef2686b57725a890268c01a86d92784704335aa
  got  b690c618f7ef137b4834057d41779be6049ad31bc5d353e616693c46213f0a44
$ echo $?
1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Download it again. A partial download or a bad mirror gives a different hash every time.
.SS Check everything in a SUMS file
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ checksum -c SHA256SUMS
OK       debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso
OK       debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-1.iso
MISSING  debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-2.iso
2 OK, 0 failed, 1 missing
$ checksum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing
OK       debian-13.1.0-amd64-netinst.iso
OK       debian-13.1.0-amd64-DVD-1.iso
2 OK, 0 failed, 1 not here and skipped
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first run exits 1 because of the missing file. The second exits 0.
.SS Compare with a hash from a web page
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ checksum notes.txt 9c345463e1fec644c6eee8e6158d953f
OK       notes.txt  (md5)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The 32 digits told \fBchecksum\fR to use md5.
.SS A Fedora CHECKSUM file
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ checksum -v Fedora-Workstation-Live-42-1.1.x86_64.iso
checksum: found Fedora-Workstation-Live-42-1.1.x86_64.iso in ./Fedora-Workstation-42-1.1-x86_64-CHECKSUM
+ sha256sum < Fedora-Workstation-Live-42-1.1.x86_64.iso
OK       Fedora-Workstation-Live-42-1.1.x86_64.iso  (sha256, Fedora-Workstation-42-1.1-x86_64-CHECKSUM)
.fi
.RE
.SS Make a SUMS file of your own
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ checksum --no-sums *.iso > SHA256SUMS
$ checksum -a md5 notes.txt ubuntu-24.04.3-desktop-amd64.iso
9c345463e1fec644c6eee8e6158d953f  notes.txt
3bee204ae8e1ed05ba6ab8e448ef40be  ubuntu-24.04.3-desktop-amd64.iso
.fi
.RE
.PP
The output has the same format as \fBsha256sum\fR, so \fBsha256sum \-c\fR reads it too.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBchecksum: the hash has 6 hex digits, a hash has 32, 40, 64 or 128\fR : Part of the hash is missing. Copy it again. When it came from a page that wraps long lines, quote it so both halves reach \fBchecksum\fR.
.PP
\fBchecksum: needs b3sum. Install it with: sudo apt install b3sum\fR : The line names the package for your distro. Install it, or use another algorithm.
.PP
\fBMISSING  debian\-13.1.0\-amd64\-DVD\-2.iso\fR : The SUMS file lists files for a whole release, and you downloaded only some. Add \fB\-\-ignore\-missing\fR.
.PP
\fBchecksum: none of the files in SHA256SUMS is here\fR : Run it from the folder that holds the files, or keep the SUMS file in that folder. \fBchecksum\fR looks for the listed names beside the SUMS file.
.PP
A plain hash instead of \fBOK\fR : No SUMS file beside the file lists it by that name. A renamed download, such as \fBdebian.iso\fR, no longer matches the name in \fBSHA256SUMS\fR. Use \fBchecksum \-c SHA256SUMS\fR after renaming it back, or compare with the hash directly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Every file matched, or it printed the hashes.
.TP
1
A hash did not match, or a file is missing or unreadable.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as a hash of the wrong length or an unknown algorithm.
.TP
3
The hash tool is missing.
.TP
4
Refused.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBdupes\fR, \fBsha256sum(1)\fR, \fBb2sum(1)\fR, \fBgpg(1)\fR
